# Search RL (template; an earlier copy ran the node-138 trial of 2026-10-20, below; the full runs are on Cluster Alpha/Beta: scripts/alpha/search.env, scripts/beta/search.env): Octopus fork/join trained on offline search questions (octopus/envs/search) by the
# code-bundle trainer. Launch on the trainer host with: RL_ENV_FILE=<copy of this file> scripts/rl_env/rl_run.sh start
# Before the start, on that host:
#  - the retrieval service of the training corpus (part A, OpenResearcher corpus) up at OCTOPUS_SEARCH_URL
#    (POST /search, POST /open, GET /health): --backend bm25, the main setting for training and BrowseComp-Plus eval;
#    search answers as BrowseComp-Plus's tool (the agent asks for the top 10, each the document's first 512 Qwen3
#    tokens: --tokenizer);
#  - a judge: an OpenAI-compatible endpoint (e.g. its own SGLang server, not one of the policy servers) at
#    OCTOPUS_JUDGE_URL serving OCTOPUS_JUDGE_MODEL, Qwen3.5-27B at temperature 0 (CLM's; octopus/envs/search/reward.py:
#    its grader prompt and parser, enable_thinking false); unset OCTOPUS_JUDGE_URL = exact match only. With
#    OCTOPUS_JUDGE_CACHE set (below) the judge must be an SGLang server exposing /model_info (the cache keys on its
#    model_path), otherwise every judged episode is rejected. A judge
#    cache written before the BrowseComp-Plus grader (keys without "bcp") is never read: start a new one;
#  - the questions as a JSONL of {qid, question, answer} (on the host, never in the repo: rl.search_questions) and the
#    training split, a file of qids (part A's split file: rl.search_split), both from
#    `python -m octopus.envs.search.data export <Gold-Doc dir> <out dir>`. Re-export a file written before #176: the
#    loader now unboxes LaTeX gold answers (\boxed{x} -> x), an older export still holds them boxed;
#  - the Harbor environment at $OCTOPUS_HARBOR (default /home/user/harbor-udocker: scripts/env.sh puts the harbor CLI
#    on PATH; each episode runs octopus.envs.search.agent with $OCTOPUS_SEARCH_PYTHON, default the python next to that
#    harbor CLI: set it when harbor is a uv tool shim, e.g. <harbor venv>/bin/python; PYTHONPATH=$CODE). No containers.
# Every <...> below is this host's: set it in the copy. Two things the node-138 trial (2026-10-20) learned:
#  - exact match alone (no OCTOPUS_JUDGE_URL) starves the zero-variance filter: at ~22% accuracy about 1 group in 5 is
#    mixed. Run with the judge, or add rl.filter_zero_variance=false to EXTRA (pilots);
#  - one A6000 SGLang server saturates (30 running / 68 queued requests, 900 s request timeouts): use >= 3-4 policy
#    servers (INFER_GPUS) for EPISODES_PER_SERVER=16. For search EPISODES_PER_SERVER counts groups, not episodes:
#    16 = 16 groups x n_samples (8) = 128 episodes per server (scripts/alpha/search.env).
# Per episode (octopus/envs/search/defaults.py, docs/search-env.md "Limits"; not repeated here): no shared token
# budget (OCTOPUS_SEARCH_BUDGET=0), each thread, main or branch, may add 32768 tokens to its context
# (settings.thread_budget, as in SWE), at most 10 branches (OCTOPUS_MAX_BRANCHES), only main forks (OCTOPUS_FORK_DEPTH=1),
# main 100 tool calls, a branch 50 turns, the last 2048 tokens of a thread's growth kept for its done; replies capped
# at 4096 tokens (octopus/envs/search/rl.MAX_NEW_TOKENS), sampled at gconfig.temperature 1.0; read_thread on
# (OCTOPUS_READ_TOOL=1, below).
# read_thread mounts need CURSOR (below: the AReaL + SGLang overlays with the cursor and mount patches, on every rollout
# host; it exports OCTOPUS_CURSOR=1) and one server per episode tree (rl_run.sh exports
# OCTOPUS_ROUTE_SERVERS, every server it started);
# without CURSOR every read is refused, nothing mounted. Never set OCTOPUS_READ_CONTROL here: it adds read credit
# (read_adv, octopus/envs/search/reward.search_read_rewards: a branch's first read gets the reader's own grade less its
# read control's, each the grade of the finding its commit hands main; a done or abort has none), a non-outcome term,
# and this template is pure outcome (below).
# Advantage: pure whole-episode reward, G = 8, R = 2y-1 (y: the main answer correct), leave-one-out over the mains, no
# std, one advantage shared by every thread of the tree, no main-first control (rl.plan_control=false).
AREAL_PY=<areal venv>/bin/python
SGLANG_PY=<sglang venv>/bin/python
CODE=<code snapshot dir>  # a code snapshot with octopus/envs/search
MODEL_TEXT=<models>/Qwen3.5-4B-text
MODEL_VL=<models>/Qwen3.5-4B
ADAPTER0=<models>/adapter-v0-4b-text-r64
FILEROOT=<areal-exp dir>
STATE=<state dir>/rl-state-search-s1
JOBS_ROOT=<harbor env>/rl  # the episodes' trial dirs: $JOBS_ROOT/jobs-TRIAL
INFER_GPUS=0,1,2
TRAIN_GPUS=3,4,5,6,7
STEPS=40
EXCLUDE=""  # code-pool only
MEM_MAX=400G
CUDA_HOME=/usr/local/cuda-12.2
EPISODES_PER_SERVER=16  # groups per server here (x n_samples episodes each), not episodes as for SWE bundles' 8
# Optional admission (docs/search-env.md "Admission"): OCTOPUS_ROUTE_KV / _WAIT, and, with OCTOPUS_ROUTE_KV > 0,
# OCTOPUS_ROUTE_TREE_TOKENS (unset = off): a server holds at most max(1, KV x pool / it) active episode trees
OFFPOLICY=2
LORA_RANK=64
LORA_KEEP=10
MEM_FRAC=0.88
MB_TOKENS=262144
export OCTOPUS_HARBOR=<harbor env> OCTOPUS_CODE=$CODE  # OCTOPUS_SEARCH_PYTHON=<harbor venv>/bin/python if harbor is a shim
export OCTOPUS_SEARCH_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8100
# read_thread on: only here (defaults.DEFAULTS has none), the episodes inherit it; rl_run.sh passes it to the REMOTE hosts
export OCTOPUS_READ_TOOL=1
export OCTOPUS_JUDGE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:30300/v1 OCTOPUS_JUDGE_MODEL=judge OCTOPUS_JUDGE_TIMEOUT=120 OCTOPUS_JUDGE_CACHE=$STATE/judge-cache.jsonl
EXTRA="workflow=octopus.envs.search.rl.SearchEpisodeAgent rl.search_questions=<data>/search/openresearcher-questions.jsonl rl.search_split=<data>/search/train-qids.txt gconfig.n_samples=8 train_dataset.batch_size=8 saver.freq_steps=5 recover.freq_steps=1"
CURSOR=<cursor overlay dir>  # docs/cursor-protocol.md "Deployment": $CURSOR/areal (logprobs + cursor + route-spread + cpu-batch + train-rpc-timeout patches, in place of AREAL_OVERLAY) and $CURSOR/sglang (cursor + mount patches)
